7. P2 — BUILD THE THREAT PRIORITY MATRIX
Goal

Prioritize realistic threats to the organization — not every threat group or every technology.
Key Rule
text

Scenario first.
Actor second.

Do not start with a giant “APT list.”
Start with the ways the organization could realistically be harmed.

Starter Threat Scenarios

    Scenario 1 — Exploitation of Public-Facing Systems
        Vulnerable VPN, firewall, remote-access service, web application, email server, edge device, or cloud/admin service.

    Scenario 2 — Phishing, Credential Theft, and Identity Compromise
        Phishing, fake login pages, password spraying, MFA abuse, stolen tokens, OAuth abuse, vendor impersonation.

    Scenario 3 — Ransomware / Access Broker Intrusion
        Stolen credentials, exposed remote services, exploitation, lateral movement, data theft, encryption, extortion.

    Scenario 4 — Espionage / Sensitive Data Theft
        Targeted access to email, cloud data, sensitive systems, research, operational data, legal data, or personnel data.

    Scenario 5 — Third-Party / Supply-Chain Compromise
        Vendor access, contractor accounts, trusted integrations, shared services, software/service-provider compromise.

    Scenario 6 — Public Disruption / Defacement / DDoS (only if relevant)
        Public website disruption, hacktivism, defacement, denial-of-service, or public-service interruption.

Threat Priority Scoring

Score each category from 1–3.
