10. P3 — MEASURE SUCCESS AND IMPROVE
Goal

Measure usefulness and outcomes — not just report volume.
Track These Metrics
text

- Number of high-priority intelligence items mapped to PIRs
- Time from signal to relevance/affected-status decision
- Number of affected-asset validations completed
- Number of patches, mitigations, hunts, detections, or blocks triggered
- Number of findings routed to an identified owner
- Number of actions completed or accepted as risk
- Stakeholder usefulness rating
- Stakeholder feedback on timeliness and relevance
- PIRs supported during the reporting period
- Threat scenarios updated due to real incidents or environmental changes

Feedback Questions
text

1. Was this timely?
2. Was this relevant to your work?
3. Did it help you make a decision or take action?
4. Was the recommended action clear?
5. What should be changed next time?

Review Rhythm

    Review urgent items after closure.
    Review PIR support monthly.
    Review stakeholder feedback monthly or quarterly.
    Review and update PIRs at least quarterly.
    Review threat scenarios when major technology, mission, or threat changes occur.

11. DO NOT START THESE YET

    Do not build a giant named-actor/APT matrix.
    Do not manually rebuild the full enterprise asset inventory.
    Do not create a threat scenario for every product or application.
    Do not enable every CTI alert or integration at once.
    Do not automate blocking or containment before validating the workflow.
    Do not send raw IOC lists without context, relevance, and ownership.
    Do not broadly monitor employees, executives, partners, or external assets without approval.
    Do not make every threat “high priority.”

12. FIRST SUCCESSFUL MILESTONE
Minimum Viable CTI Workflow
text

1. Stakeholder / action-owner map exists.
2. Three to five PIRs are documented.
3. Critical technology / asset watchlist exists.
4. Five or fewer threat scenarios are prioritized.
5. One threat signal is taken through:
   signal → relevance → affected check → owner → action → closure.
6. Feedback is collected and the workflow is improved.

